#uuid "e99d8d22-f103-5560-a608-35f0957133f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065131 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065132 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065132 #edb/ref 1061 188375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065132 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a7e8a41f-1f30-5c54-b22b-c51e7a5efd82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065132 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065133 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065133 #edb/ref 1061 188379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065133 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "12b96179-bf32-5136-bbf1-e7631a656dfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065133 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065134 #edb/ref 1061 188380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065134 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9f2475d1-01ac-524a-9a5e-a1adfdc797a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065134 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065281 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065281 #edb/ref 1061 77819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065281 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39aa10e9-422a-5c1c-b5b2-de119dcca067" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065281 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065282 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065282 #edb/ref 1061 77820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065282 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46d3cb51-86b4-59d6-8a39-eab709bb05a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065282 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065283 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065283 #edb/ref 1061 77824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065283 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b2792086-a352-5ca3-b086-f381a58864aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065283 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065284 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065284 #edb/ref 1061 77825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065284 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "503ce197-02ae-5ae2-897a-80af7de15a2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065284 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065285 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065285 #edb/ref 1061 77828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065285 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b129822d-f637-5611-9cad-e2f8b6aefca4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065285 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065286 #edb/ref 1061 77829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065286 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ddc832d-59f9-5fa2-85e2-002f7b9c4ccc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065286 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065293 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065293 #edb/ref 1061 82238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065293 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "480bb98f-29a4-57e1-9342-972680f415f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065293 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065294 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065294 #edb/ref 1061 82239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065294 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cb9bb839-e0dc-5aca-9cc9-ae22d07a3486" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065294 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065295 #edb/ref 1061 82242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065295 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b387ed41-0522-5959-a882-fcd13a445bf5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065295 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065361 #edb/ref 1064 "Multiplying any number by zero *always* equals zero. Below are some examples.\n\n- $5 \\times 0 = 0$\n- $123\\times 0 = 0$\n- $-314\\,159 \\times 0 = 0$\n- $0\\times 0 = 0$\n\nTo see *why* multiplying any number by zero always equals zero, let's take a look at the multiplication table for $5$, shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 \\cdot 4 &= 20 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 3 &= 15 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 2 &= 10 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 1 &= 5 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 0 &= ◻\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is reduced by $5$ each time. Following this pattern, we must have\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}\\square = 0\n$$\n\n.\n\nNote that, since multiplication is commutative, multiplying zero by any number (including itself) equals zero as well. Some examples are shown below.\n\n- $0 \\times 5 = 0$\n- $0 \\times 123 = 0$\n- $0 \\times -314\\,159 = 0$\n- $0 \\times 0 = 0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065361 #edb/ref 1065 3119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065361 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7ed778da-8d5a-5363-972b-d9435e22a0e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065361 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065363 #edb/ref 1064 "Dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero. For example:\n\n- $0 \\div 2 = 0$\n- $0\\div (-456) = 0$\n\nTo understand why dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero, think about it this way: if you have zero pizzas, and you want to divide zero pizzas among ten friends, then each friend will get exactly zero pizza." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065363 #edb/ref 1065 3120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065363 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "65c0e3ec-7962-5911-a3c9-9de126e87edc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065363 #edb/ref 1067 "Dividing Zero by Another Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065364 #edb/ref 1064 "Division by zero is **undefined.** No number can be divided by zero, not even zero itself.\n\nTo see why, notice that if we divide $6$ by $3$, we get\n\n$$\n6\\div 3 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n6 = 3\\times 2\n$$\n\nHowever, this argument does not work if we divide by zero. Suppose we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThen, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nand this relationship cannot be true because any number multiplied by zero is zero! Therefore, $$? is not a number." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065364 #edb/ref 1065 3121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065364 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d8152649-f215-53b2-9811-ce74f0d61dc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065364 #edb/ref 1067 "Dividing by Zero is Undefined" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065412 #edb/ref 1064 "When we subtract a positive number from another positive number, we move towards the left on the number line. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following subtraction problem:\n\n$$\n3 - 5\n$$\n\nTo subtract $5$ from $3,$ we start at $3$ on the number line and move $5$ places to the left.\n\n![](images/57/57ac1fe7a28ae5fefadf900d2276d499cdccf01df48b819b0aafa5347a2ec506.png)\n\nWhen we do this, we land at $-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n3-5 = -2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065412 #edb/ref 1065 8029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065412 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8af27dde-6aa4-5067-84f8-9538f1acaa18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065412 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065413 #edb/ref 1064 "It is impractical to use number lines to subtract large numbers. \n\nHowever, a neat trick makes subtracting a positive number from a smaller positive number very easy!\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following subtraction problem:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{37}} - {\\color{blue}{68}}\n$$\n\nSince $68$ is larger than $37,$ the answer *must be negative*.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem: subtracting the numbers *in the reverse order*. In our case, the reverse problem is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{68}} - {\\color{red}{37}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\n![](images/db/db192e5c1f089e3d4cc2a7b1238392e44a7ba36bff99345a997042c16ebc304f.svg)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{68}} - {\\color{red}{37}} = 31.\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{37}} - {\\color{blue}{68}} = -31.\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! \n\nLet's use the same idea to subtract two three-digit numbers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065413 #edb/ref 1065 8030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065413 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d7dbb60e-47d5-56db-981a-145c532f7ace" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065413 #edb/ref 1067 "Subtraction With Larger Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065415 #edb/ref 1064 "Multiplying with negative numbers can be summed up by two simple rules.\n\nThe first rule says that when we multiply a negative number by a positive number, the result (**product**) is a negative number. We can picture this multiplication rule as\n\n$$\n(-)\\cdot(+) = (-)or(+)\\cdot(-) = (-)\n$$\n\nFor instance, let's find out the value of $(-3) \\cdot 10$.\nHere, we have a product of a negative number $\\color{red}(-3)$ and a positive one ${\\color{blue}(10)}$. So the product will be negative:\n$30$\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply $3$ by $10$ without any signs:\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot 10 = 30\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-3)} \\cdot {\\color{blue}10} = {\\color{red} -30}\n$$\n\nNote that multiplication is **commutative**, meaning it doesn't matter in what order we multiply two numbers. Commutativity means that we're allowed to swap the numbers, which gives\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-3)} \\cdot {\\color{blue}10} = {\\color{blue}10}\\cdot {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{red} -30}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065415 #edb/ref 1065 8105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065415 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1eb02261-90e4-51bc-a24e-dabdedaafa63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065415 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065416 #edb/ref 1064 "Dividing with negative numbers follows similar rules as multiplying with negative numbers. \n\nFirst of all, a negative number divided by a positive number gives a negative number. We can picture this rule as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,÷\\,(+)\\,=\\,(-).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's use this rule to find the value of $(-6) \\div 2.$\n\nHere, since we are dividing a negative number $\\color{red}(-6)$ by a positive number ${\\color{blue}(2)},$ the result is going to be negative:\n![](images/b6/b64108fa03fc286ddcd04ac4cd7a9d77c1672d0ced66ebb1953c97afa5488533.svg)\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $6$ by $2$ *without* any signs: $6 \\div 2 = 3.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-6)} \\div {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{red} -3 }.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065416 #edb/ref 1065 8108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065416 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b92ac2e4-b983-5fd5-bc18-7dfb821497f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065416 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065417 #edb/ref 1064 "A positive number divided by a negative number is also a negative number. We can picture this rule as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(+)\\,÷\\,(-)\\,=\\,(-).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, to find $8\\div (-2),$ we have to divide a positive number $\\color{blue}(8)$ by a negative one ${\\color{red}(-2)}.$ So the result will be negative:\n![](images/79/797eda7126fff569fccda2681c628f99f103057d7c5e23c1b65cb566b48ef203.svg)\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $8$ by $2$ without any signs: $8 \\div 2 = 4.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} \\div {\\color{red}(-2)} = {\\color{red}-4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065417 #edb/ref 1065 8109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065417 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "67beb927-7da4-5576-8982-1a823c878691" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065417 #edb/ref 1067 "The Quotient of a Positive and a Negative is a Negative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065418 #edb/ref 1064 "Finally, the third rule is that a negative number divided by a negative number is a positive number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,÷\\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $-12 \\div (-3).$ We have to divide a negative number $\\color{red}(-12)$ by another negative number ${\\color{red}(-3)}.$ So the result will be positive:\n![](images/d3/d31776de6ab95f8178d0bdd6e3b34cfa2f4fc0b987851dcace50aff1818936da.svg)\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $12$ by $3$ without any signs: $12 \\div 3 = 4.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-12)} \\div {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{blue}4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065418 #edb/ref 1065 8111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065418 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b1d3d95e-7953-5c5b-b97b-d4eb85e1f80d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065418 #edb/ref 1067 "The Quotient of a Negative and a Negative is a Positive" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072476 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186072477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072476 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186072478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072476 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186072479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072476 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186072480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072476 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186072481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072476 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072476 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072476 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186072479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072476 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4ad60804-e649-58c3-9918-6d89f3cb7178" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072477 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072477 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072477 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9cf06951-cbcc-525c-9842-0890581834c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072478 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072478 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072478 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b04ecb02-3d9b-5237-9855-274124892856" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072479 #edb/ref 1080 "1" 